            <div class="tab">Abstract</div>
            <section class="abstract">
                <p>The Sun appears almost perfectly spherical because of the balance between gravity and internal pressure acting throughout the star. Gravity pulls solar material inward equally from all directions, while the pressure generated by nuclear fusion in the core pushes outward.</p>
                <p>This balance creates a shape that is close to a perfect sphere. Although the Sun rotates, its rotation speed is relatively slow compared to its size, so the distortion is extremely small. This article examines the physical processes responsible for the Sun’s shape, including gravity, hydrostatic equilibrium, and rotational forces.</p>
            </section>

            <div class="body">
                <h2 class="section-title">Introduction</h2>
                <p>The Sun is the largest object in the Solar System and contains more than 99 percent of its total mass. When observed from Earth or from space-based telescopes, the Sun appears nearly perfectly round. This spherical shape is not accidental, but rather the result of fundamental physical forces acting inside the star.</p>
                <p>In astronomy, large celestial bodies tend to become spherical because gravity pulls matter inward equally in all directions. Once an object reaches sufficient mass, gravity overcomes the strength of the material composing the object and forces it into a rounded shape. This process is known as hydrostatic equilibrium. The Sun, being made mostly of hot plasma rather than solid material, is especially capable of forming a smooth and symmetric structure.</p>
                <p>However, the Sun is not mathematically perfect. Because it rotates on its axis, centrifugal forces slightly reduce the inward pull of gravity at the equator. This causes a very small equatorial bulge. Compared to planets such as Jupiter or Saturn, which rotate rapidly and are visibly flattened, the Sun rotates slowly enough that its deviation from a perfect sphere is extremely small.</p>

                <h2 class="section-title">Methodology</h2>
                <p>This article uses a theoretical and observational approach based on established principles of astrophysics. Scientific concepts including gravity, hydrostatic equilibrium, plasma behavior, and rotational mechanics are examined to explain the Sun’s shape.</p>
                <p>Observational evidence from solar imaging and measurements of solar oblateness are used to support the discussion. Solar oblateness refers to the difference between the Sun’s equatorial and polar diameters. Data collected from space observatories and helioseismology studies help determine how closely the Sun resembles a perfect sphere.</p>
                <p>Comparisons are also made between the Sun and other rotating celestial bodies, including planets and rapidly rotating stars, in order to understand the effects of rotational velocity on shape deformation.</p>

                <h2 class="section-title">Discussion</h2>
                <p>The primary reason the Sun is spherical is gravity. Gravity acts toward the center of mass of an object. In a massive body such as the Sun, this inward force pulls material equally from all directions. Over time, this naturally produces a shape where all points on the surface are approximately the same distance from the center. A sphere is the most stable configuration for such a system.</p>
                <p>Inside the Sun, nuclear fusion in the core generates enormous amounts of energy. This energy creates outward pressure as extremely hot plasma pushes away from the center. The balance between inward gravitational force and outward pressure is known as hydrostatic equilibrium. Without this balance, the Sun would either collapse inward under gravity or expand outward uncontrollably.</p>
                <p>Although gravity strongly favors a spherical shape, rotation introduces another force into the system. As the Sun rotates, centrifugal effects slightly oppose gravity at the equator. However, the Sun rotates relatively slowly — approximately once every 25 days at the equator — so the resulting deformation is minimal.</p>

                <h2 class="section-title">Conclusion</h2>
                <p>The Sun is almost perfectly spherical because gravity pulls solar material inward equally from all directions while internal pressure generated by nuclear fusion pushes outward. This balance, called hydrostatic equilibrium, produces a stable and nearly symmetric shape. The Sun’s plasma composition allows matter to flow and redistribute efficiently, further supporting spherical symmetry.</p>
                <p>Although the Sun rotates, its rotational speed is not high enough to create significant flattening. As a result, only a very small equatorial bulge exists. Compared with rapidly rotating planets and stars, the Sun remains remarkably close to a perfect sphere. The study of the Sun’s shape demonstrates how fundamental physical laws govern the structure of celestial objects.</p>
